    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "One Word Substitution replaces a lengthy phrase with a single precise term to enhance brevity and clarity in language.",
        "Learning Latin and Greek root words, prefixes, and suffixes allows students to decode unfamiliar terms systematically.",
        "Vocabulary in this section is commonly categorized into thematic groups like phobias, manias, forms of government, and personal traits.",
        "Regular revision of previous years' questions (PYQs) is essential as high-frequency words recur often across competitive exams."
      ],
      "id": "9gu6f2y"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Distinguish carefully between subtle nuances in related terms, such as 'Atheist' (disbeliever in God) vs. 'Agnostic' (uncertain about God).",
        "Common root words like 'Omni' (all), 'Phil' (love), 'Mis' (hate), and 'Cide' (killing) are extremely high-yield for exam preparation.",
        "Questions frequently test categories related to governance (e.g., 'Anarchy', 'Oligarchy', 'Plutocracy') and belief systems."
      ],
      "id": "y4q0mq9"
    },
